  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 242 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge currently over the Great Plains shifts towards \nthe Mississippi Valley by Tuesday with the ridge axis eventually \nbecoming centered across the region. Meanwhile, the aforementioned \nlongwave trough out west will be propagating into the Desert \nSouthwest and interacting with the remnants of Hurricane Polo. As \nthis happens, the return of deep southerly flow encompasses the area \nleading to warm/moist advection, mostly from the eastern Pacific. \nThe most noticeable change to our sensible weather on Tuesday will \nbe the return of warmer temperatures areawide, with highs expected \nto be in the upper 70s/mid 80s, a 10 degree increase from Monday. \nMoisture will be a bit slower to return, with the most anomalous \nvalues staying across western MO and the Great Plains Tuesday into \nWednesday. Temperatures by Wednesday afternoon should be warmer for \nmost the area, as southwesterly low-level flow increases, leading to \nhighs in the 80s everywhere except northeast MO, where the best rain \nchances (30-40%) are forecast. Tuesday and Wednesday will feature an \nabundance of rainfall to the west across the Plains, with a steady \neastward progression of the widespread rainfall axis into our area \nWednesday night into Thursday.\n\nGuidance continues to indicate an anomalous axis of moisture (PWATs \n>95th percentile) being advected northward from the Pacific, with \ngeneral consensus in the greatest moisture arriving Thursday-Friday. \nThis is when most of the area should experience widespread, \npotentially beneficial rainfall. The LREF mean accumulated 24hr \nprecipitation probabilities for QPF >0.50\" continues to reveal 40-\n50% values, illustrating a modest chance of substantial rainfall. \nRainfall amounts up to 3\"+ are certainly possible for some by next \nweekend, per the LREF accumulated precip 90th percentile. With the \ngreatest mid/upper level synoptic forcing for ascent and highest \ncolumn moisture both west of the area, it appears the best chance \nfor amounts like that should be across the northern/western half of \nMO and west-central IL. Rain chances are expected to end for most by \nthe weekend as long-range guidance indicates a cold front and \ntrailing surface high sliding south into the region, pushing out the \nanomalous moisture. This should also work to bring in cooler more \nseasonable air, as LREF IQRs reveal spreads of 5 degrees centered on \nvalues near normal by next weekend.",
